{
  // Stricter typechecking for the pure domain core AND anything built on it
  // (POLYTONIC-PLAN §2.1: core/ and adapters/).
  //
  // The root tsconfig.json runs `strict: false` for the legacy tree, where
  // turning on strictNullChecks today surfaces 64 pre-existing errors. That
  // migration is worth doing and is not this change.
  //
  // But core/ cannot wait for it: discriminated-union narrowing — the thing
  // that makes `Result<T, E>` safe to use rather than merely documented —
  // only works when strictNullChecks is on. Without it TypeScript never
  // narrows `if (result.ok)`, so every consumer of a Result would need a cast
  // and the type would be decorative.
  //
  // adapters/ belongs in this same project, not the root one, for a concrete
  // reason found while wiring up adapters/ingest/markdown.mjs: TypeScript
  // typechecks a file's full import graph, not just what an `include` glob
  // matches. The moment an adapter imports core/shared/result.mjs, the root
  // (non-strict) project pulls that core file in transitively and fails on
  // it — the same narrowing gap this file exists to fix, now showing up one
  // layer removed. Since every adapter exists specifically to consume core/'s
  // Result-typed contracts, adapters/ needs the same strict settings core/
  // does; splitting them into two configs would just reproduce this failure
  // for the next adapter that imports anything from core/.
  //
  // So the core AND its adapters opt into the stricter regime from day one,
  // and the legacy tree migrates on its own schedule. New code is held to the
  // higher bar; old code is not blocked on it.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "checkJs": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitAny": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": false
  },
  // The same rule extends to FLAT-ROOT FAÇADE scripts that consume core/.
  // Path stability (#1386) keeps entry points at the repo root, so a script
  // like cv-coverage.mjs lives beside the legacy tree while importing core/ —
  // and the root project's `"*.mjs"` glob would otherwise pull core/ in
  // transitively and fail on exactly the narrowing gap described above.
  //
  // Each such file is listed here AND excluded from tsconfig.json's include.
  // The rule for anything added later: if a root-level script imports from
  // core/ or adapters/, it belongs in this list, not the root project.
  "include": ["core/**/*.mjs", "adapters/**/*.mjs", "cv-coverage.mjs"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "core/**/*.test.mjs", "adapters/**/*.test.mjs"]
}
